# Hansen (2015): Punishment and Deterrence in Drunk Driving

Benjamin Hansen, "Punishment and Deterrence: Evidence from Drunk Driving," *American Economic Review* 105(4): 1581–1617, 2015.

## The question

Hansen asks a simple question: does harsher punishment stop people from committing the same crime again? Economic theory says it should, but this is hard to test. People who get harsher punishments are usually different from people who get lighter ones, so you can't just compare the two groups. Drunk driving is a good place to test it. In Washington State, the punishment depends on a breath test number, not on a judge's opinion. The punishments are mostly fines, short jail stays and losing your licence. None of these keeps someone off the road for long, so if people reoffend less, it is likely because they were deterred. The question also matters for policy. In 2013 the NTSB proposed lowering the legal limit from 0.08 to 0.05, and Hansen's results speak to whether that would work.

## What was believed before

Before this paper, economists had good evidence that crime falls when people are more likely to get caught, for example when there are more police or more speed checks (Levitt 1997; DeAngelo and Hansen 2014). The evidence that harsher punishment reduces crime was much weaker and mixed. Some studies found an effect, but others found that young offenders barely respond to tougher adult penalties (Lee and McCrary 2009), or that harsher prisons make people reoffend more (Chen and Shapiro 2007). A major review later summed this up: crime clearly responds to policing, but there is far less evidence that it responds to the severity of punishment (Chalfin and McCrary 2017). Earlier drunk-driving studies mostly compared states before and after they changed their legal limit (Dee 2001). Those studies show the effect of moving the limit, but not the effect of the punishment itself on the person who gets it.

## Identification strategy and the assumption it rests on

Hansen uses a regression discontinuity design. Each driver's breath test gives a blood alcohol content (BAC) reading, and the law sets punishment based on that number. At 0.08 the driver is charged with DUI, which brings a fine, at least a day in jail, a 90-day licence suspension and tougher penalties for any future offence. At 0.15 the charge becomes aggravated DUI, which brings a bigger fine, more jail time and a one-year licence revocation, but no change in future penalties. Hansen compares drivers just below each cutoff with drivers just above it, and checks whether they are stopped for drunk driving again within four years. In practice he fits a separate straight line on each side of the cutoff, using drivers within 0.05 of it. The jump between the two lines at the cutoff is the effect of the harsher punishment.

The key assumption is that drivers just above and just below the cutoff are the same in every way except their punishment. If nothing changed at 0.08, reoffending would rise or fall smoothly across it with no jump. This only works if no one can control which side of the line they land on. Hansen argues that drivers cannot predict their BAC to three decimal places, and that the reading comes from a machine, not an officer. He backs this up with evidence. There is no unusual bunching of drivers just under either cutoff. Drivers on both sides look the same in age, sex, race, accident history and past offences. His results also hold when he drops the drivers closest to each cutoff.

The two cutoffs should not be expected to give the same answer. They compare different drivers (people near 0.08 versus heavier drinkers near 0.15), and they change different things. Crossing 0.08 raises both today's punishment and future penalties, while crossing 0.15 only raises today's punishment. Hansen uses this difference to separate the effect of fearing future punishment from the effect of being punished now.

## What it changed

The paper gave strong evidence that harsher punishment can reduce repeat offending, which the earlier literature had struggled to show. Crossing 0.08 cut reoffending by about 2 percentage points, or about 17%, and the effect grew over time. That suggests people changed their behaviour, rather than just being kept off the road for a while. The 0.15 cutoff showed that being punished more right now matters even when future penalties stay the same. This idea, called specific deterrence, was later studied directly by others. The paper also changed the policy conversation. Hansen estimates that lowering the limit to 0.05 would save at most 120 to 200 lives a year, far fewer than the NTSB predicted. He argues it would work better to make punishment rise steadily with BAC, since the most dangerous drivers are well above the limit.

## Reception and criticism

The paper is widely cited and is now a standard textbook example of regression discontinuity (Cunningham 2021). The criticism falls into four areas. First, Hinnerich, Pettersson-Lidbom and Priks (2016) point out that fines, jail, licence loss and future penalties all change at the same cutoff, so Hansen cannot say which punishment actually works. Using Swedish data where only the type of sentence changes, they find that a short, mild prison sentence cuts reoffending by about 30%. Second, Gehrsitz (2017) notes that Hansen measures the effect of being charged, not of actually receiving any one punishment, so the true effect is probably larger. In German speeding data where he can see who actually lost their licence, he finds a one-month suspension cuts reoffending by about 20%. Third, BAC is only recorded in steps of 0.001, and Hansen groups his standard errors by these steps. Kolesár and Rothe (2018) show that this common method can make results look more certain than they really are. That doesn't overturn Hansen's main results, but his confidence intervals are probably too narrow. Fourth, the public dataset used in class has 214,558 stops, only part of Hansen's roughly 513,000 tests. Cunningham (2021) says this data can only approximate Hansen's tables, not fully replicate them. Overall, most researchers accept that the cutoffs work as a natural experiment (Sloan 2020). The open questions are about which punishment drives the effect and whether the results apply beyond Washington.

